  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Saturday)...\n\nSatellite imagery shows an upper low over southeastern  \nSaskatchewan and cyclonic flow over a broad region including \nsouthern Alberta, Montana and northeast Wyoming. There is a an \nupper low off the Pac NW coast pushing moisture into the Rockies. \nWebcams showed some light snow or flurries over the \nAbsarokas/Beartooths while radar indicated possible flurries in \nthe foothills and as far east as Yellowstone County.\n\nLight snow showers will linger over the mountains thanks to the\nlingering cyclonic flow over our region. As 500 mb heights \nbuild into the area by morning from the southwest we will see \nsome isentropic lift spread into our CWA along with some Pacific \nmoisture on top of low level light/easterly winds. This may \nproduce scattered flurries over our foothills and nearby lower \nelevations...but nothing of impact. Most models do not show any \nmeasurable precip outside of the Absarokas/Beartooths, which \nshould see snow showers and an inch or less of accumulation. PoPs \nand weather have been adjusted accordingly. Otherwise, most of the\nregion will be dry/cool today. Another weak shortwave tracks \nthrough the region Monday bringing snow showers mainly to the \nmountains, but strongest forcing is expected to drive well to our \nsouth so do not expect any real impacts. Generally speaking, look \nfor below normal temps, light winds and patchy nighttime fog \nthrough Monday. Today looks like the coldest day with many \nlocations likely to stay below 30F.\n\nAs 500 mb heights rebound again Monday night into Tuesday, models\nsuggest lee side troughing will increase the pressure gradient\nacross the Paradise Valley and foothills of south central MT.  \nThus, we continue to anticipate SW-W winds to increase in these \nareas late Monday night. In fact, this pattern may favor gusts \nnearing 50 mph at the gap locations by daybreak Tuesday. This is \nsupported by the gradient and stability, but mid level flow is \nexpected to be modest (~30kts at 700mb). After some decrease \nduring the day Tuesday, expect the gradient to tighten again\nTuesday night into early Wednesday. We will need to monitor winds\nacross the foothills through the rest of the week as well, as a \ndry anticyclonic upper pattern is forecast by the ensembles to\nprevail. \n\nBottom line, the week ahead is looking mostly dry with windy\nperiods in the foothills possibly impacting high profile vehicles. \nRegarding temperatures, look for low-mid 40s by Tuesday climbing \ninto the 50s by the end of the week. Thanksgiving is looking \nwarmer than normal and potentially breezy. BT",
